---
export const prerender = true;
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Card from '../components/ui/Card.astro';
---

<Layout title="How it works — Recipe Nutrition Calculator">
	<section class="max-w-[760px] mx-auto px-md sm:px-lg py-3xl space-y-lg">
		<div>
			<h1 class="text-display-lg text-ink mb-sm">How it works</h1>
			<p class="text-body-md text-body">
				We'd rather be transparent about how the numbers are calculated than pretend they're more precise
				than they are.
			</p>
		</div>

		<Card variant="marketing">
			<h2 class="text-display-sm text-ink mb-xs">Where the nutrition data comes from</h2>
			<p class="text-body-md text-body">
				Nutrition values come from a curated database of common ingredients, built from public-domain USDA
				reference data. It's not a live lookup against the full USDA database — it's a focused set covering
				the ingredients that show up in the vast majority of home recipes. If an ingredient isn't in our
				database, we fall back to a generic estimate and flag it clearly as{' '}
				<strong>approximate</strong> rather than guessing silently.
			</p>
		</Card>

		<Card variant="marketing">
			<h2 class="text-display-sm text-ink mb-xs">How we parse your recipe</h2>
			<p class="text-body-md text-body">
				Pasted text (and photo transcriptions) are split into ingredient lines and parsed for quantity, unit,
				and ingredient name — handling fractions, ranges, and common unit abbreviations. Each ingredient name
				is then matched against our database using word-overlap scoring. A low-confidence match is shown as{' '}
				<strong>low-confidence</strong> on the ingredient line so you know to double-check it. If an ingredient
				isn't in our database at all, we ask an AI model for a best-guess estimate rather than skipping it —
				those lines are clearly marked <strong>AI-estimated</strong>, since that number isn't sourced from
				verified nutrition data.
			</p>
		</Card>

		<Card variant="marketing">
			<h2 class="text-display-sm text-ink mb-xs">How units convert to grams</h2>
			<p class="text-body-md text-body">
				Weight units (grams, ounces, pounds) convert directly. Volume units (cups, tablespoons) use an
				ingredient-specific density where we have one — flour and water don't weigh the same per cup — and
				count units ("2 eggs", "1 onion") use an average weight for that ingredient where known. When we
				don't have ingredient-specific data, we fall back to water's density for volume or a generic
				per-item weight for counts, and mark that line <strong>approximate</strong>.
			</p>
		</Card>

		<Card variant="marketing">
			<h2 class="text-display-sm text-ink mb-xs">URL import</h2>
			<p class="text-body-md text-body">
				We look for the structured "Recipe" data (schema.org JSON-LD) that most recipe sites embed for
				Google's recipe search results. If a site doesn't publish that data, URL import won't work for it —
				paste the ingredients directly instead.
			</p>
		</Card>

		<Card variant="marketing">
			<h2 class="text-display-sm text-ink mb-xs">The health score</h2>
			<p class="text-body-md text-body">
				A simple, transparent 1–10 score starting at 5: it goes down for high sodium, saturated fat, or sugar
				per serving (over 20% of the daily value), and up for a good source of fiber or protein (over 15–20%
				of the daily value). It's a rough signal, not a clinical assessment.
			</p>
		</Card>
	</section>
</Layout>
